{
  // The single type-check baseline, and it is read by **both** tools: Pylance (the editor) reads
  // ``pyrightconfig.json``, and basedpyright — the CI gate — reads it too, with precedence over
  // ``[tool.basedpyright]``.
  //
  // It lives here rather than in ``pyproject.toml`` because of a measured trap: adding a
  // ``[tool.pyright]`` section there makes basedpyright ignore its own section *and* the pyright one,
  // falling back to its defaults — ``recommended`` over the whole workspace, 661 errors where there
  // were none. That would have broken the gate while looking like an editor-only change.
  // ``pyrightconfig.json`` is the one file both tools honour, so the editor and the gate cannot drift
  // apart; an editor that reports what CI never checks is what teaches people to ignore red.
  //
  // ``testing`` is in scope on purpose. It was out while the suite carried 47 diagnostics, and
  // clearing them paid for itself: a diagnostic of exactly that kind is what found
  // ``testing/evaluation/hierarchy_proposal.py`` broken — a script the suite does not cover, so
  // nothing else would have. The baseline stays ``basic``: this is a gate, not a style contest.
  //
  // ``extraPaths`` is what makes ``from testing.conftest import ...`` resolvable: the tests import the
  // suite as a package rooted at the repository, which pytest arranges and a type checker does not.
  //
  // ``docs/_hooks`` is in scope because it is code that runs outside the package — the MkDocs hook
  // that produces the documentation freshness report (ADR 0010). A file that runs in CI but sits
  // outside the gate is exactly the one that rots; it is a lone module, not a package.
  "typeCheckingMode": "basic",
  "pythonVersion": "3.12",
  "include": ["src/scrinalia", "main.py", "testing", "docs/_hooks"],
  "exclude": ["**/__pycache__", ".venv", "migrations", "Data"],
  "reportMissingTypeStubs": false,
  "extraPaths": ["."]
}
